WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.280
Part two. You will hear a podcast by a running coach giving advice about taking up running

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:15.600
and giving information about her club. First, you have some time to look at questions eleven

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.440
to fourteen.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:44.840
Now listen carefully and answer questions eleven to fourteen.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:50.400
My name's Liz Fuller and I'm a running coach with Compton Park Runners Club. Welcome to

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.759
my podcast. If you're thinking about taking up running, I'm here to help. There are many

00:00:56.759 --> 00:01:02.000
training programmes available online which aim to help people build up to running five

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.199
kilometres. Some of them are great and thousands of people of all ages are taking part in five-kilometre

00:01:10.199 --> 00:01:16.559
races across the country as a result. People like them because they're easy to follow and

00:01:16.559 --> 00:01:22.720
don't push them too hard. However, they don't work for everyone, especially if you suffer

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.519
from something like a heart condition or asthma, because they're aimed at people with average

00:01:28.519 --> 00:01:34.879
fitness and running ability. Another thing is that everyone is different and if you have

00:01:34.879 --> 00:01:42.480
any specific questions related to your needs, there's no one to provide any answers. I have

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.839
a couple of simple tips I always give to new runners. I expect you've been told to run

00:01:48.839 --> 00:01:56.480
very slowly until your fitness increases. Well, I find that can prevent progress. You

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:03.120
should run at a speed that feels comfortable, but time yourself and try to run a bit faster

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.800
each time. Listening to music can be very helpful. It takes your mind off things and

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.960
helps your body get into a rhythm. I'd say that is better than running with a friend,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.440
especially as most people are competitive and that's not what you want when you're

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:27.440
just starting. I don't think the time of day is especially important. Some people are better

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:34.520
in the evening, while others are morning people. But you need to be consistent, so aim to train

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.559
regularly. Twice a week is enough to begin with.

00:02:37.559 --> 00:02:47.559
Before you hear the rest of the podcast, you have some time to look at questions 15 to 20.

00:03:07.559 --> 00:03:25.559
Now listen and answer questions 15 to 20.

00:03:25.559 --> 00:03:30.160
New members often say to me that they've been put off running either because they lack

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:37.479
confidence or they don't have time or they think they dislike running. Kerry, for example,

00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:43.440
joined the club two years ago at the age of 40. She'd always enjoyed running at school

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.000
but wasn't sure if she'd be able to do it. She was worried about being left behind and

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:56.119
being the slowest runner. But she says she was made to feel so welcome she soon forgot

00:03:56.119 --> 00:04:02.800
all about that. James had always hated the idea of running, but a friend encouraged him

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.800
to come along for a taster session and he hasn't looked back. He never misses a training

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:15.479
session despite having a really demanding job. Leo was worried about having to commit

00:04:15.479 --> 00:04:21.000
himself to training sessions every week and wasn't sure he'd be able to fit training

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:28.399
into his busy schedule. But after experiencing a lot of stress at work, he came along to

00:04:28.399 --> 00:04:34.679
us and gave it a go. Now he says he feels much more relaxed and he looks forward to

00:04:34.679 --> 00:04:42.299
his weekly run. Mark is quite typical of our new members. He's never considered himself

00:04:42.299 --> 00:04:47.679
to be a sporty person and it was only when he retired that he decided to take up the

00:04:47.679 --> 00:04:53.980
challenge of trying to run five kilometres. It took him months to find the courage to

00:04:53.980 --> 00:05:00.420
contact us but felt reassured immediately as there were other people his age who were

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:08.459
only just taking up running for the first time. My own journey hasn't been easy. I

00:05:08.459 --> 00:05:15.420
did my first marathon when I was 37 after having had two kids. My husband had been running

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:21.660
marathons for years but I never dreamed I'd be doing one with him. I managed to complete

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:28.380
it in four hours but I felt like giving up halfway through. It was only the support of

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:34.700
the spectators that kept me going. I do think signing up for a race of whatever length is

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:42.059
motivating, whether it's 5k or 25k, because it's good to have something to work towards

00:05:42.059 --> 00:05:48.540
and it gives you a sense of achievement. I did my first 10k after only six months which

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:54.619
was certainly very challenging and not something I'd necessarily recommend. But after you've

00:05:54.619 --> 00:06:00.019
been training for a few weeks it's worth putting your name down for a 5k. Some people

00:06:00.019 --> 00:06:05.420
find they only need a few practice runs before taking part in a race but I'd give yourself

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:11.619
a couple of months at least. Well, I hope that's given you a really good idea of what

00:06:11.619 --> 00:06:22.140
to sign up for.

